      if (todo.editing) li.classList.add('editing');

      todoList.appendChild(li);
    });
    // ...
}
```

OK so the logic seems correct — when `todo.editing` is true, the `editing` class is added to the `li`. But the issue says "`<input class="edit" value="feed the cat">` is not visible because it has CSS property: `display: none`". This means the `li` doesn't have the `editing` class when the test tries to interact with the edit input.

Let me look at the dblclick handler:

```javascript
li.querySelector('label').addEventListener('dblclick', function () {
    todo.editing = true;
    render();
    var editInput = li.querySelector('.edit');
    editInput.focus();
    editInput.selectionStart = editInput.selectionEnd = editInput.value.length;
});
```

After `render()` is called, `li` is now a detached DOM node (since `render()` rebuilt the list). The code then tries to `li.querySelector('.edit')` on the old `li` which is no longer in the DOM. The actual edit input in the DOM is in the new `li`, not the old one.

